          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              How much floor space does a folded treadmill really need by
              room type?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              A bedroom corner or closet typically fits the most compact
              folded units, roughly <strong>27-32 inches wide</strong>. A
              living room or den usually has room for mid-size folded
              footprints up to about 35 inches wide. A dedicated home gym or
              garage can accommodate any folded size, including the larger
              frames that come with tall-runner and family-focused machines.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Is a folding treadmill worth buying if I might move within a
              year or two?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              For most renters expecting to move, yes. A folding treadmill is
              dramatically easier to relocate than a fixed-frame machine,
              generally fitting through standard doorways without
              disassembly and rolling on its own transport wheels to a moving
              truck, which meaningfully lowers the practical cost and hassle
              of ownership even if you relocate sooner than planned.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What's the real difference between a hydraulic fold and a
              manual latch?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              A hydraulic or gas-strut fold does most of the lifting work for
              you and controls the deck's descent so it lowers slowly instead
              of dropping, which matters most if you fold daily or have back
              sensitivity. A manual latch is perfectly safe when engaged
              properly but takes more physical effort and requires you to
              control the descent yourself.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Can a taller person use a compact folding treadmill
              comfortably?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              It depends heavily on deck length rather than folded footprint.
              Taller runners, generally over about <strong>6 feet</strong>,
              tend to feel cramped on decks shorter than 55-60 inches, so
              it's worth prioritizing a longer deck over an ultra-compact
              folded size if height and stride length are a concern.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do budget folding treadmills under $500 sacrifice safety
              features?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Not necessarily. Core safety features like a folding safety
              latch and an emergency stop clip are standard even on budget
              machines. What typically gets scaled back at lower prices is
              motor power, deck cushioning, and weight capacity headroom,
              rather than the fundamental safety mechanisms themselves.
            </p>
          </div>
